<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berthold V, Duke of Zähringen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1218">1218</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berthold V was the last duke of Zähringen, a powerful dynasty of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, ruling from 1186 to 1218. He founded the city of Bern in 1191 to strengthen his hold on the lands between the Jura and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and declined the German crown offered to him in 1198. With his death in 1218 the main Zähringen line died out.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berthold-v-duke-of-zahringen.jpg" alt="Berthold V, Duke of Zähringen" width="276" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Zell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berthold_IV,_Duke_of_Z%C3%A4hringen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Berthold, also known as Bertold or Berchtold, was born in 1160, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berthold-iv-of-zahringen">Berthold IV, Duke of Zähringen</a>, and Heilwig of Frohburg, and succeeded his father in 1186. Early in his reign he reduced the power of the Burgundian nobles and settled the Bernese Oberland and the area of Lucerne. He enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thun">Thun</a>, and Bern, founded in 1191, became the focus of his expansion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of Emperor Henry VI in 1198, Berthold was one of the candidates in the imperial election. He offered his nephews as hostages to the Archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> to win their support, but when he found that a majority had chosen the Hohenstaufen Philip of Swabia, he renounced his claim. In exchange he received territorial concessions in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, consolidating Zähringen control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ortenau">Ortenau</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a>, Schaffhausen, Breisach and All Saints' Abbey, and Philip paid him 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marks.</p><p>In the same year Berthold crushed a rising of the Burgundian nobles, an event recorded on the gate at Breisach. In 1200 he began rebuilding the parish church of Freiburg in the Romanesque style; continued in Gothic from around 1240, it became Freiburg Minster. His attempt to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> failed at the Battle of Ulrichen in 1211, and the treaty signed at Hautcret Abbey on 19 October 1211 forced him to accept Savoyard suzerainty over the upper Valais.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Berthold married Clementia, daughter of Stephen III of Auxonne. Their son Berthold died before his father, in 1216, so when Berthold V died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a> on 18 February 1218 the Zähringen line became extinct in the male line. The family's lands partly reverted to the crown, were granted imperial immediacy, or were divided between the houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Urach</a>, whose members became the counts of Freiburg, Kyburg and Fürstenberg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berthold V of Zähringen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Berthold V of Zähringen</b> founded Bern on a loop of the Aare in 1191.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-bern">Canton of Bern</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.03,7.88&amp;year=1208" aria-label="House of Zähringen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4734 483 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Berthold V, Duke of Zähringen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5184 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5184 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.03,7.88&amp;year=1208">What was here in 1208? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
